0
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:06,090
We all regularly drive the car.

1
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:09,410
We want to get from point A to point B. Usually,

2
00:00:09,410 --> 00:00:11,960
we like to drive with a constant speed.

3
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:13,950
But what happens if there's a traffic light?

4
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:16,650
Well, we have to stop eventually,

5
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:18,680
which means we have to brake, and that

6
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:21,180
makes the car decelerate.

7
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:24,540
So there are different parts of motion when we ride a car.

8
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:25,630
It can be constant.

9
00:00:25,630 --> 00:00:27,170
It can be accelerating.

10
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:28,970
It can be decelerating.

11
00:00:28,970 --> 00:00:32,910
So let's consider this in a little graphic.

12
00:00:32,910 --> 00:00:38,650
We have a little car here, and it drives with constant speed

13
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:42,980
until someone slams into the brakes,

14
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:44,988
and the car will eventually stop.

15
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:50,660
Let's add a timeline to this.

16
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:54,920
Here we're going to put t equals 0.

17
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:58,360
The point in time where the car stops

18
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:02,600
driving with a constant speed is t1,

19
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:07,430
and the point where the car has come to a complete stop is t2.

20
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:14,630
And the problem now asks for, what is the position function?

21
00:01:14,630 --> 00:01:18,050
What is the distance at any kind of time here

22
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:22,590
as the car goes through these two different phases of motion?

23
00:01:22,590 --> 00:01:27,690
So ultimately, we want to get to x of t.

24
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:31,160
But we can't actually just write this up.

25
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:34,320
We need to first look at other information

26
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:37,730
that we have about this kind of set up.

27
00:01:37,730 --> 00:01:40,065
And what we have is information on the acceleration.

28
00:01:44,490 --> 00:01:47,790
And we have to consider these two phases separately.

29
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:53,720
So we have here phase 1 and phase 2.

30
00:01:53,720 --> 00:01:58,750
And phase 1 is from 0 to t1.

31
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:04,130
And phase 2 is from t1 to t2.

32
00:02:04,130 --> 00:02:08,060
And we know when a car is going with a constant velocity,

33
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:11,030
its acceleration is going to be 0.

34
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:14,460
So that keeps life easy for phase 1.

35
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:17,890
And then when it brakes, well, we know it decelerates,

36
00:02:17,890 --> 00:02:20,790
so we're definitely going to have a minus sign.

37
00:02:20,790 --> 00:02:26,240
And it brakes between t and t1, and we

38
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:30,860
need to multiply this factor here with a constant.

39
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:35,210
So this is our acceleration information

40
00:02:35,210 --> 00:02:38,850
that the problem has given us.

41
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:41,930
And we know, of course, that if we integrate the acceleration,

42
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:44,280
we're going to get to the velocity function.

43
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:45,880
And if we integrate that one again,

44
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:48,860
we're going to get to our position function.

45
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:52,862
So let's do that first for phase 1.

46
00:02:56,010 --> 00:02:57,940
And what is also important here is

47
00:02:57,940 --> 00:02:59,700
that we need to carefully consider

48
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:01,290
the initial conditions.

49
00:03:01,290 --> 00:03:04,310
Again, what additional information

50
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:07,480
is this problem giving us?

51
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:12,580
So initial conditions, they always make life a lot easier.

52
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:16,770
So it's important to carefully look for them.

53
00:03:16,770 --> 00:03:18,930
And what that means is we want to know

54
00:03:18,930 --> 00:03:23,620
what the distance is at t equals 0

55
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:28,530
and what the velocity at time 0 is.

56
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:31,490
And we know the distance here is 0,

57
00:03:31,490 --> 00:03:36,680
and we know that the problem starts when the car is already

58
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:38,980
driving with a constant speed.

59
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:41,800
So that's going to be v0.

60
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:45,930
So let's then make use of this and start

61
00:03:45,930 --> 00:03:47,619
integrating our acceleration.

62
00:03:47,619 --> 00:03:48,910
We want to get to the velocity.

63
00:03:48,910 --> 00:03:56,210
So v of t minus v of t equals 0 is going to be that integral.

64
00:03:56,210 --> 00:04:02,270
And here already we know that v of t equals 0 is the v0.

65
00:04:02,270 --> 00:04:05,390
So this already looks pretty nice.

66
00:04:05,390 --> 00:04:09,456
And now we have our integral here of a.

67
00:04:09,456 --> 00:04:12,230
And, of course, now t is an integration variable,

68
00:04:12,230 --> 00:04:14,490
so we have to give this a prime, and we're

69
00:04:14,490 --> 00:04:21,360
integrating from t prime equals 0 to t prime equals t.

70
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:25,100
And when we plug this in, well, a

71
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:28,150
is 0 in phase 1, which means actually our integral is

72
00:04:28,150 --> 00:04:29,890
going to be 0.

73
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:31,940
That's handy.

74
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:39,320
And from that, we will find that v of t equal simply v0.

75
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:41,120
All good.

76
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:46,220
Now we need to integrate this to get to our position function.

77
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:53,460
Same thing again-- x of t minus x of t equals 0.

78
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:57,010
Let's quickly check here what we know about x of t0.

79
00:04:57,010 --> 00:05:00,530
Well, it's 0 from the initial conditions,

80
00:05:00,530 --> 00:05:06,080
and now we have to integrate our velocity function here,

81
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:11,960
v0 dt, again, prime.

82
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:13,840
t prime equals 0.

83
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:15,270
t prime equals t.

84
00:05:15,270 --> 00:05:18,800
These are our boundaries.

85
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:26,300
And this is simply going to be v0t prime evaluated from 0

86
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:28,390
to t.

87
00:05:28,390 --> 00:05:35,810
And we can add that in there, and it's just going to be vt.

88
00:05:35,810 --> 00:05:40,040
So x of t-- I can just write it here again. x of if t

89
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:41,630
equals v0t.

90
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:45,920
Good.

91
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:51,540
So half of the problem is solved.

92
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:57,770
Let's look at the same things for phase 2.

93
00:05:57,770 --> 00:06:01,550
So let's consider the initial conditions for phase 2 now.

94
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:08,470
And again, now we want to, in the analogous way,

95
00:06:08,470 --> 00:06:14,160
look at the distance of what we know about the distance at t1

96
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:16,950
and the velocity a t1.

97
00:06:16,950 --> 00:06:21,090
And about the position, we know from over there,

98
00:06:21,090 --> 00:06:23,180
that this is v0t.

99
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:25,220
So our previous result now actually becomes

100
00:06:25,220 --> 00:06:27,670
the initial condition for the next step, right?

101
00:06:27,670 --> 00:06:30,410
Because whatever happens here sets

102
00:06:30,410 --> 00:06:33,110
whatever happens afterwards.

103
00:06:33,110 --> 00:06:35,020
So that's nice.

104
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:38,290
And we know that up to this moment

105
00:06:38,290 --> 00:06:43,180
here, this car goes with a velocity of v0.

